title: Daniel Merrill, A Forgotten Baptist
description: How Daniel Merrill, the Congregational pastor of Sedgwick, Maine, studied baptism and led his church to become Baptist in 1805, and what he later wrote of baptized churches and Baptist origins.
output: lessons/history/daniel-merrill.html

# From the notes (studies/data/notes/daniel-merrill-notes.html), in the author's words, arranged on slides.
# The notes' closing link ("See the slides: Early American Baptists") is the read: link on the last slide.

=== Introduction
--- Daniel Merrill [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Daniel Merrill
sub: A Forgotten Baptist
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides.

--- A convinced pedobaptist
kicker: 1765–1833
lead: Daniel Merrill (1765-1833) pastored the Sedgwick Congregational Church, the largest church in Maine. He came from a family of ministers and was a convinced pedobaptist.

=== Sedgwick
--- Some Baptists came to his town
step: 1802 | However in 1802 some Baptists came to his town. Merrill began to earnestly study the doctrine of baptism. Soon he was convinced the Bible only taught believer’s immersion. He shared the results of his study with his congregation in several sermons.
step: Feb. 28, 1805 | On February 28, 1805, the church voted unanimously to become a Baptist church. They sent for help from the Baptists of New England.
step: May 13, 1805 | On May 13, 1805, in the waters of the Benjamin River, Merrill, his wife and sixty-four of their number were baptized by Thomas Baldwin of Boston and Elisha Williams of Beverly, Massachusetts.
step: The next day | The next day, nineteen more were baptized and the First Baptist Church of Sedgwick was officially constituted.

=== The rest of the story
--- A staunch Baptist
lead: While some students of Baptist history know this part, very few know the rest of the story.
p: Merrill’s studies of the Scriptures led him to become a staunch Baptist. So strong that he wrote:
quote: We purposely exclude from our communion all Churches, which admit to their community any of the unbaptized: for all such pollute, if not destroy, the Church of God, and are not baptized Churches; but are Churches, or Societies, of spurious origin; or Churches bewitched... the Paedobaptist Churches are NOT of the visible Church of Christ. | Daniel Merrill, *The Kingdom of Heaven Distinguished from Babylon*, 1808

--- At Jordan or Enon
p: Not only that, but in his 1812 *Miniature History of the Baptists*, Merrill wrote,
quote: The origin of the Baptists can be found no where unless it be conceded that it was at Jordan or Enon. | Daniel Merrill, *Miniature History of the Baptists*, 1812
p: He goes on to write of the Anabaptists, the Waldenses, and the Petrobrusians.

=== Close
--- Study his life [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Study his life
center: Those who ignorantly claim such Baptist views originated with J.R. Graves or G.H. Orchard would do well to study the life and writings of Daniel Merrill.
